    add('Executive conclusion',f'**No generic L candidate is qualified for V2 integration. Do not replace production L.** Candidate {best} is best among the three research families, but development next-3 quality-availability Brier is {num(comp["macro"]["development"][best])}, versus {num(comp["macro"]["development"]["current-derived"])} for the actual Phase 9B formula with disjoint derived inputs: **{-comp["relativeDevelopmentImprovement"]*100:.2f}% worse**. It improves contextual agreement and descriptive recovery versus that formula, yet still loses the neutral-L frozen B.1 baseline on contextual Top1/MRR. A contested-color counterexample also exposes a confidence defect.\n\nProduction `contextual-pick-score-v1.6.2`, k500, FRA sparse prior, Q/A/D, commitment, tiers, memory, WGC/localization and overlays remain unchanged. All {val["preservedFiles"]} protected files match. MH3/DFT remain untouched; BLB/DSK remain spent. Tested commit `{val["commit"]}` plus Phase C working-tree changes on 2026-10-14. [Validation](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/validation.json).')
    add('Phase C goal','Infer relative color availability from cards observed through the current pick. Quality asks how good a card is; openness asks how unexpectedly late it appears. High quality alone cannot produce a late-surprise signal. The model API consumes `PickTimeState`; future offers, selected-current-card labels, final builds and records are joined only after every prediction.\n\nPreregistered as `25578d5`, using the existing B.1 whole-set split and selected target drafts. A prospective sample-confidence clarification was committed as `781c4e3` before temporal/development analysis. No set-specific archetype rules or black-box model were introduced. [Plan](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/preregistration.json).')
    add('Current production L audit','`late-color-lane-v1` is already a generic heuristic. For each positively supported colored occurrence: `clip((pick-ALSA)/3,0,1) * clip((k500-adjusted GIH-baseline)/.04,0,1) * clip((pick-3)/5,0,1)`. It splits gold evidence by color count, uses pack weights 1/.5/.25, confidence `min(eligiblePositivePositions/4,1)`, centers evidence over five colors, clips relative support by scale2, and uses the minimum constituent support for gold-card fit. It has no within-pack age decay or explicit negative/absence evidence. P1P1–P1P3 provide zero positive signal.\n\nPremier/Traditional adjustment limit is .020, Quick .010. Production score L divides by the human maximum, so Quick contributes half the Premier lane range. This study replays Premier only; VOW Quick is position-audit evidence, not Quick L integration proof. No production configuration changes.\n\nB.1 snapshots have ALSA/ATA null, so their original L is neutral. We compare that frozen baseline separately from `current-derived`: the **actual** `LaneOpennessProfile.Analyze` and `StatisticalRecommendationEngine` receive global seen means from disjoint evidence drafts, while only their L output is substituted into frozen Q/A/D. Derived averages are explicitly not official provider ALSA. [Implementation](src/DraftTG.RecommendationEngine/LaneOpennessProfile.cs).')
    cal=[r for r in audit if r['set'] in ('DMU','BRO','WOE') and r['format']=='PremierDraft' and not r['packSpecific'] and r['lambdaDrafts']==0]
    add('Derived position feature quality',f'Recomputed **{load(OUT/"position-provenance.json")["datasets"]} calibration/development set-format datasets**, with B.1 target drafts excluded. The saved full Phase B histograms were not used as model inputs because they contain target/self-future positions. Source zero-based coordinates become 1-based **pack-local** positions; global source index remains separate. Offered multiplicity defines Seen; actual picked occurrences define Taken.\n\nCalibration seen-position forecast errors (independent fit/audit halves inside evidence drafts):\n\n'+table(['Estimator','Mean card/pack absolute error (picks)'],[(method,num(statistics.mean(r[field] for r in cal))) for method,field in (('mean','meanMAE'),('median','medianMAE'),('10% trimmed mean','trimmedMAE'))])+f'\n\nGlobal empirical CDF CRPS {num(policy["calibrationCRPS"])} beats pack-specific CDF {num(next(x["CRPS"] for x in policy["scoreGrid"] if x["packSpecific"] and x["lambdaDrafts"]==0))}. Median/trimmed mean can improve point MAE; this does not prove superior lane prediction. A/B retain interpretable mean-based families, while C evaluates the full CDF. Taken forecasts are separately audited against held taken positions, with missing training groups explicit. No claim mean is universally best. Same-set mean-position percentiles are recomputed from evidence drafts and checked across independent fit/audit halves; they are descriptors, not lane inputs. [Percentile stability](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/position-percentile-audit.json).\n\nPer-set/format, pack, sample count, rarity, color and available Q strata are in [seen audit](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/position-audit.json) and [taken audit](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/taken-position-audit.json). The neutral-or-lower Q bucket includes neutral estimates for unavailable evidence; older-set/Quick Q is unavailable and cannot support measured-quality inference. Rarity is diagnostic metadata, never a lane bonus; logical identities do not recover every original printing rarity.')
    add('Position confidence','Forecast pooling and signal uncertainty are separate. Calibration CDF CRPS selected zero pooling toward a broad set distribution, preserving card-specific CDFs. Explicit user requirements still require sample-sensitive lane confidence. A prospective clarification therefore fits positive penalties 20/100/400 jointly with temporal policies; every selected family uses:\n\n`c_card = n_independent_evidence_drafts_seen / (n_independent_evidence_drafts_seen + 20)`\n\nTwenty independent seen drafts yield .5, 10,000 would yield .998; 10,000 sightings from repeated cards are not 10,000 independent drafts. Seen/Taken counts, variance and histograms remain visible. Rolling confidence is `N_effective_observed_support/(N_effective_observed_support+4)`, with recency and pack retention applied. This is an evidence-budget heuristic, **not a calibrated posterior that a color is open**. The contested-color probe reveals why that distinction matters. [Clarification](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/confidence-clarification.json).')
    add('Card-level surprise','A uses positive raw lateness `clip((p-mu_seen)/3,0,1)`. B uses signed standardized lateness `clip((p-mu_seen)/max(1,SD_seen),-1,1)`. C uses signed empirical mid-CDF rank `2*(F_seen(p)-.5*mass_seen(p))-1`. Each is multiplied by card confidence, bounded quality eligibility and a pick ramp. All selected explicit negative weights are zero.\n\nA card with high Q at its expected or earlier position contributes no positive A signal; a weak late card has zero eligibility. C is the percentile of an observed sighting, **not physical-pack survival probability**: original pack IDs, copy identities, initial collation and wheel provenance are unavailable. Missing card expectation contributes no evidence; a missing qualified positional dataset declines the entire experimental substitution and retains existing L.')
    add('Quality gating','`g = clip(2*(frozen_current_raw_Q-.5),0,1)`. This emphasizes above-baseline cards without changing Q. It never uses experimental k250, future target-game outcomes, rarity or final records. It uses intrinsic Q rather than committed-deck relevance, avoiding turning lane inference into a confirmation of already chosen colors.\n\nThe k500 evidence snapshots and all Q provenance/sparse-prior contracts remain frozen. These are mature-format replay snapshots, not proven historical publication-time statistics; no chronological availability or live-draft improvement claim is made.')
    add('Negative evidence','Signed B/C variants tested explicit negative coefficients 0 and .25. Calibration chose **0** for every family. We do not turn unseen cards into invented disappearance observations. Lack of fresh qualified evidence causes aging; relative centering can lower unsupported colors. Early negative observations may also consume the per-observation normalization budget even when their explicit accumulator coefficient is zero.\n\nThis conservative choice has a demonstrated failure: earlier-than-expected qualified sightings can increase coverage-style confidence while old positive evidence remains. Blue support rises in the controlled probe despite contested-color evidence. All candidates remain unqualified; absence inference and supporting-evidence confidence need repair. [Probes](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/behavior-probes.json).')
    add('Color aggregation','Per observation, positive/negative color vectors sum confidence × quality eligibility × positional surprise × occurrence quantity × pick ramp. Divide by `max(1,totalPositive+abs(totalNegative))` to bound each observation. Aging banks accumulate vectors; retained evidence is centered over W/U/B/R/G, then `support_c = tanh((evidence_c-meanEvidence)/1.5) * rollingConfidence`, in [-1,1].\n\nThis is relative openness: equally supported colors are neutral, not all declared closed. One occurrence cannot provide unbounded evidence. Supporting positions are proxies; repeated sightings are not proven independent physical packs/cards. The coverage-confidence defect prevents treating the accumulator as a Bayesian certainty estimate.')
    add('Multicolor handling','Gold evidence splits evenly and receives an additional .5 discount. Offline per-card L uses the minimum constituent support: `.5+.5*min(colorSupport)`. This prevents a UR card from receiving a strong lane bonus from U alone. Colorless cards and lands contribute no generic color evidence and get neutral experimental L. No archetype/synergy inference or fixing-land lane bonus was added.')
    add('Recency','All selected families use exponential aging `2^(-source_global_pick_gap/16)`. Global source indices preserve actual historical pack lengths rather than inventing 42-pick chronology. Every stored pack bank ages each observation, including gaps. The pick ramp is `clip((packLocalPick-2)/6,0,1)`, so P1P1/P1P2 are neutral and P1P3 is weak.\n\nTemporal fit compared half-lives 4/8/16 on calibration only. The long selected memory helps measured future availability but also contributes to the contested-color failure. Stability/responsiveness tables and reversal labels remain descriptive, not guarantees of real-table inference.')
    add('Pack-direction behavior','P1/P3 are one passing direction; P2 is opposite. Banks are separate by pack. The current bank has weight1; older same-direction banks weight .5; opposite-direction retention tested 0/.25/.5 and selected .5, in addition to global aging. Thus P2 reduces old neighbor certainty, while P3 can reuse a decayed P1 belief. The fitted data did not establish a smaller opposite-direction retention than the fixed same-direction .5. No evidence is carried unchanged across boundaries.')
    add('Candidate A/B/C formulas',table(['Family','Surprise','Confidence / aging / retention','Explicit negative coefficient'],[(f,'positive raw lateness' if f=='A' else 'standardized signed lateness' if f=='B' else 'signed empirical mid-CDF',f'n/(n+20); half-life16; old-pack .5',temporal['selected'][f]['config']['negative']) for f in ('A','B','C')])+'\n\nCommon quality gate, ramp, observation cap, gold policy, centered tanh support, missing-data decline and bounded L conversion apply. With fitted negative coefficients0, B/C accumulate their positive parts. Parameters are shared across sets, not hand-authored by color/archetype. Family parameters were fitted on calibration; family selection uses development primary Brier, not human picks or wins. [Calibration grid](docs/evidence/v2-phase-c/temporal-calibration-corrected.json).')
